[{"type":"text","value":"Required"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Source vertical position"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Channel<TDatum, ChartKey?>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One group"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Independently triangulated source group"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Channel<TDatum, ChartKey>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Inferred"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stable point identity used in edge keys"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"string"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Layer-derived"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stable mark ID"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Channel<DelaunayLinkDatum, ChartKey?>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Edge group"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Edge value sent to the color scale"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stroke"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"VisualChannel<DelaunayLinkDatum, string>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resolved color"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Final stroke"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"strokeOpacity"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"strokeWidth"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"VisualChannel<DelaunayLinkDatum, number>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Link defaults"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Edge presentation"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"strokeDasharray"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lineCap"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"curve"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Native "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"link"},{"type":"text","value":" presentation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Link defaults"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Segment presentation"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"motion"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartMotionDefinition<DelaunayLinkDatum>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Chart policy"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Edge enter, update, and exit motion"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"z"},{"type":"text","value":" is evaluated on source rows before layout. Rows with the same non-null\ngroup are triangulated together; links never cross groups. Presentation\naccessors are evaluated on the derived edge datum, so they can inspect both\nendpoints explicitly:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"delaunayLink(rows, {\n  x: 'x',\n  y: 'y',\n  stroke: ({ source, target }) =>\n    source.category === target.category ? '#2563eb' : '#94a3b8',\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"final-screen-topology","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Final-screen topology"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Initial complete x/y pairs establish the positional domains. After scales and\ninner bounds resolve, the mark projects both axes and triangulates those pixel\npositions. It then renders semantic endpoints through the native "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"link"},{"type":"text","value":" mark."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This ordering matters because anisotropic x/y ranges can change which diagonal\nis Delaunay. Resizing may therefore change adjacency even when the source rows\nand domains are unchanged. Repeated compilation at the same dimensions is\ndeterministic. Inversion is not required, so continuous, temporal, and\ncategorical positional scales are supported."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"edge-lineage-and-identity","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Edge lineage and identity"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DelaunayLinkDatum<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>"},{"type":"text","value":" contains:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"source"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"target"},{"type":"text","value":", retaining both original row objects;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sourceIndex"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"targetIndex"},{"type":"text","value":", retaining their original input indexes;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sourceKey"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"targetKey"},{"type":"text","value":";"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"semantic "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x1"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y1"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x2"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y2"},{"type":"text","value":" endpoints;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"group"},{"type":"text","value":"; and"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a canonical "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"edgeKey"},{"type":"text","value":" derived from the endpoint keys."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Supply "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"},{"type":"text","value":" when source order can change. Point keys also provide deterministic\ntie-breaking for cocircular and coincident positions. Endpoint order and edge\nkeys remain stable across responsive topology updates. The interaction point is\nthe native link midpoint and retains the complete edge datum for tooltips and\nmotion."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Missing or invalid x/y pairs are omitted without expanding either positional\ndomain. Coincident points do not produce zero-length edges. Zero or one unique\nposition produces no links, two positions produce one link, and collinear\npositions connect consecutive neighbors."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"visible-links-versus-nearest-point-lookup","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Visible links versus nearest-point lookup"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"delaunayLink"},{"type":"text","value":" when adjacency is part of the visible encoding. If Delaunay\nis used only to accelerate focus lookup, supply a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartSpatialIndexFactory"},{"type":"text","value":"\ninstead; an invisible link layer should not change focus candidates or bundle\ncost."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"options","text":"Options","level":2},{"id":"final-screen-topology","text":"Final-screen topology","level":2},{"id":"edge-lineage-and-identity","text":"Edge lineage and identity","level":2},{"id":"visible-links-versus-nearest-point-lookup","text":"Visible links versus nearest-point lookup","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n`delaunayLink` connects observations that are neighbors in a Delaunay\ntriangulation of their final scaled screen positions. Import it from the\noptional spatial subpath; it is not included in the root or universal barrel.\n\n```ts\nimport { delaunayLink } from '@tanstack/charts/spatial/delaunay'\n\ndelaunayLink(cars, {\n  x: 'weight',\n  y: 'economy',\n  key: 'id',\n  stroke: '#94a3b8',\n  strokeOpacity: 0.75,\n  strokeWidth: 1,\n})\n```\n\n```ts\nfunction delaunayLink<TDatum, TOptions extends DelaunayLinkOptions<TDatum>>(\n  source: Iterable<TDatum>,\n  options: TOptions,\n): ChartMark<DelaunayLinkDatum<TDatum>>\n```\n\nThe public type surface includes `DelaunayLinkOptions` and\n`DelaunayLinkDatum`.\n\n## Options\n\n| Option                                    | Type                                       | Default        | Meaning                                 |\n| ----------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------ | -------------- | --------------------------------------- |\n| `x`                                       | `Channel<TDatum, ChartValue?>`             | Required       | Source horizontal position              |\n| `y`                                       | `Channel<TDatum, ChartValue?>`             | Required       | Source vertical position                |\n| `z`                                       | `Channel<TDatum, ChartKey?>`               | One group      | Independently triangulated source group |\n| `key`                                     | `Channel<TDatum, ChartKey>`                | Inferred       | Stable point identity used in edge keys |\n| `id`                                      | `string`                                   | Layer-derived  | Stable mark ID                          |\n| `color`                                   | `Channel<DelaunayLinkDatum, ChartKey?>`    | Edge group     | Edge value sent to the color scale      |\n| `stroke`                                  | `VisualChannel<DelaunayLinkDatum, string>` | Resolved color | Final stroke                            |\n| `strokeOpacity` and `strokeWidth`         | `VisualChannel<DelaunayLinkDatum, number>` | Link defaults  | Edge presentation                       |\n| `strokeDasharray`, `lineCap`, and `curve` | Native `link` presentation                 | Link defaults  | Segment presentation                    |\n| `motion`                                  | `ChartMotionDefinition<DelaunayLinkDatum>` | Chart policy   | Edge enter, update, and exit motion     |\n\n`z` is evaluated on source rows before layout. Rows with the same non-null\ngroup are triangulated together; links never cross groups. Presentation\naccessors are evaluated on the derived edge datum, so they can inspect both\nendpoints explicitly:\n\n```ts\ndelaunayLink(rows, {\n  x: 'x',\n  y: 'y',\n  stroke: ({ source, target }) =>\n    source.category === target.category ? '#2563eb' : '#94a3b8',\n})\n```\n\n## Final-screen topology\n\nInitial complete x/y pairs establish the positional domains. After scales and\ninner bounds resolve, the mark projects both axes and triangulates those pixel\npositions. It then renders semantic endpoints through the native `link` mark.\n\nThis ordering matters because anisotropic x/y ranges can change which diagonal\nis Delaunay. Resizing may therefore change adjacency even when the source rows\nand domains are unchanged. Repeated compilation at the same dimensions is\ndeterministic. Inversion is not required, so continuous, temporal, and\ncategorical positional scales are supported.\n\n## Edge lineage and identity\n\nEach `DelaunayLinkDatum<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>` contains:\n\n- `source` and `target`, retaining both original row objects;\n- `sourceIndex` and `targetIndex`, retaining their original input indexes;\n- `sourceKey` and `targetKey`;\n- semantic `x1`, `y1`, `x2`, and `y2` endpoints;\n- `group`; and\n- a canonical `edgeKey` derived from the endpoint keys.\n\nSupply `key` when source order can change. Point keys also provide deterministic\ntie-breaking for cocircular and coincident positions. Endpoint order and edge\nkeys remain stable across responsive topology updates. The interaction point is\nthe native link midpoint and retains the complete edge datum for tooltips and\nmotion.\n\nMissing or invalid x/y pairs are omitted without expanding either positional\ndomain. Coincident points do not produce zero-length edges. Zero or one unique\nposition produces no links, two positions produce one link, and collinear\npositions connect consecutive neighbors.\n\n## Visible links versus nearest-point lookup\n\nUse `delaunayLink` when adjacency is part of the visible encoding. If Delaunay\nis used only to accelerate focus lookup, supply a `ChartSpatialIndexFactory`\ninstead; an invisible link layer should not change focus candidates or bundle\ncost.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Delaunay Link Mark\ndescription: Reference for responsive Delaunay adjacency links, source grouping, endpoint lineage, stable keys, and the optional spatial import.\n---\n\n`delaunayLink` connects observations that are neighbors in a Delaunay\ntriangulation of their final scaled screen positions. Import it from the\noptional spatial subpath; it is not included in the root or universal barrel.\n\n```ts\nimport { delaunayLink } from '@tanstack/charts/spatial/delaunay'\n\ndelaunayLink(cars, {\n  x: 'weight',\n  y: 'economy',\n  key: 'id',\n  stroke: '#94a3b8',\n  strokeOpacity: 0.75,\n  strokeWidth: 1,\n})\n```\n\n```ts\nfunction delaunayLink<TDatum, TOptions extends DelaunayLinkOptions<TDatum>>(\n  source: Iterable<TDatum>,\n  options: TOptions,\n): ChartMark<DelaunayLinkDatum<TDatum>>\n```\n\nThe public type surface includes `DelaunayLinkOptions` and\n`DelaunayLinkDatum`.\n\n## Options\n\n| Option                                    | Type                                       | Default        | Meaning                                 |\n| ----------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------ | -------------- | --------------------------------------- |\n| `x`                                       | `Channel<TDatum, ChartValue?>`             | Required       | Source horizontal position              |\n| `y`                                       | `Channel<TDatum, ChartValue?>`             | Required       | Source vertical position                |\n| `z`                                       | `Channel<TDatum, ChartKey?>`               | One group      | Independently triangulated source group |\n| `key`                                     | `Channel<TDatum, ChartKey>`                | Inferred       | Stable point identity used in edge keys |\n| `id`                                      | `string`                                   | Layer-derived  | Stable mark ID                          |\n| `color`                                   | `Channel<DelaunayLinkDatum, ChartKey?>`    | Edge group     | Edge value sent to the color scale      |\n| `stroke`                                  | `VisualChannel<DelaunayLinkDatum, string>` | Resolved color | Final stroke                            |\n| `strokeOpacity` and `strokeWidth`         | `VisualChannel<DelaunayLinkDatum, number>` | Link defaults  | Edge presentation                       |\n| `strokeDasharray`, `lineCap`, and `curve` | Native `link` presentation                 | Link defaults  | Segment presentation                    |\n| `motion`                                  | `ChartMotionDefinition<DelaunayLinkDatum>` | Chart policy   | Edge enter, update, and exit motion     |\n\n`z` is evaluated on source rows before layout. Rows with the same non-null\ngroup are triangulated together; links never cross groups. Presentation\naccessors are evaluated on the derived edge datum, so they can inspect both\nendpoints explicitly:\n\n```ts\ndelaunayLink(rows, {\n  x: 'x',\n  y: 'y',\n  stroke: ({ source, target }) =>\n    source.category === target.category ? '#2563eb' : '#94a3b8',\n})\n```\n\n## Final-screen topology\n\nInitial complete x/y pairs establish the positional domains. After scales and\ninner bounds resolve, the mark projects both axes and triangulates those pixel\npositions. It then renders semantic endpoints through the native `link` mark.\n\nThis ordering matters because anisotropic x/y ranges can change which diagonal\nis Delaunay. Resizing may therefore change adjacency even when the source rows\nand domains are unchanged. Repeated compilation at the same dimensions is\ndeterministic. Inversion is not required, so continuous, temporal, and\ncategorical positional scales are supported.\n\n## Edge lineage and identity\n\nEach `DelaunayLinkDatum<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>` contains:\n\n- `source` and `target`, retaining both original row objects;\n- `sourceIndex` and `targetIndex`, retaining their original input indexes;\n- `sourceKey` and `targetKey`;\n- semantic `x1`, `y1`, `x2`, and `y2` endpoints;\n- `group`; and\n- a canonical `edgeKey` derived from the endpoint keys.\n\nSupply `key` when source order can change. Point keys also provide deterministic\ntie-breaking for cocircular and coincident positions. Endpoint order and edge\nkeys remain stable across responsive topology updates. The interaction point is\nthe native link midpoint and retains the complete edge datum for tooltips and\nmotion.\n\nMissing or invalid x/y pairs are omitted without expanding either positional\ndomain. Coincident points do not produce zero-length edges. Zero or one unique\nposition produces no links, two positions produce one link, and collinear\npositions connect consecutive neighbors.\n\n## Visible links versus nearest-point lookup\n\nUse `delaunayLink` when adjacency is part of the visible encoding. If Delaunay\nis used only to accelerate focus lookup, supply a `ChartSpatialIndexFactory`\ninstead; an invisible link layer should not change focus candidates or bundle\ncost.\n"}
